    "title": "Prologue",
    "dateline": "Fort du Taureau, Baie de Morlaix, 1871",
    "part": null,
    "html": "<p>The cell has one window, high up, and on clear nights it shows him a strip of sky about the width of his hand. Tonight is clear. He has moved the table so the lamp won’t wash out the stars, and he writes with his back to the door. The guards look in on the hour. He prefers not to see their faces.</p>\n<p>He is sixty-six. He has spent more than half his adult life in rooms like this one. Two hundred miles away, beyond the walls and the sea, the city he meant to change is being taken back street by street; the guards leave their newspapers about, and he reads them. His chest is bad again. The damp comes off the granite in the mornings and gets into the paper, and he writes small to make it last.</p>\n<p>He is writing about the stars.</p>\n<p>The argument is nearly finished, and it is simple, once you have seen it. A finite number of elements. An infinite number of worlds. Every combination used up, and then used again, without end. Somewhere at this moment another man in another fort is writing this sentence by the same lamp, in the same coat, with the same worn cuff that catches on the edge of the table. Somewhere another Paris did not fall. Somewhere he was not arrested on the eve of it.</p>\n<p>He has written that this is a consolation, and he meant it when he wrote it. We keep company, on billions of earths, with the people we have loved and lost. Then he reads it back, and stops at the phrase about tasting happiness under the figure of a double, and cannot decide whether it is a comfort or not, and writes the question in where the answer was.</p>\n<p>There is one more thing. He has been avoiding it since supper.</p>\n<p>If the doubles are real, each of them is making his mistakes. Each of them trusted the men he trusted. Each of them will be taken the night before. He knows now, in this cell, what he did not know then, and he could tell them. He could spare them all of it. He has only to find some way of writing to them.</p>\n<p>The lamp smokes. He trims the wick with his fingers, and burns them, and swears, and sucks the burn.</p>\n<p>Then he dips the pen and writes it plainly, so that no one will take it for wit.</p>\n<p class=\"set\"><i>On ne peut pas s’avertir.</i></p>\n<p class=\"set\">We cannot warn ourselves.</p>\n<p>Above him the stars go on in their combinations. He blots the line and turns the page.</p>",
